1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,284
Can you show me ConfigMaps?

2
00:00:07,833 --> 00:00:13,652
A ConfigMap is a Kubernetes object that holds configuration as key–value pairs:

3
00:00:13,652 --> 00:00:17,482
a greeting, a log level, a whole configuration file.

4
00:00:17,482 --> 00:00:23,153
Pods read it as environment variables or as files mounted into the container.

5
00:00:23,153 --> 00:00:28,604
The image stays the same; only the ConfigMap changes between environments.

6
00:00:28,604 --> 00:00:29,414
An analogy:

7
00:00:29,414 --> 00:00:37,147
the image is a printed book, the ConfigMap is the sticky note you put inside the cover ("today's special:

8
00:00:37,147 --> 00:00:37,663
soup").

9
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:42,477
If configuration lives inside the image, every change (a URL,

10
00:00:42,477 --> 00:00:45,912
a feature switch, a log level) needs a new build,

11
00:00:45,912 --> 00:00:50,820
and the same image cannot run in dev and prod with different settings.

12
00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:57,830
The twelve-factor rule "store config in the environment" is what ConfigMaps implement in Kubernetes:

13
00:00:57,830 --> 00:01:00,495
build once, configure per environment.

14
00:01:02,233 --> 00:01:04,653
And how does it actually work?

15
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:12,871
As environment variables (env.valueFrom.configMapKeyRef for one key, envFrom.configMapRef for all keys):

16
00:01:12,871 --> 00:01:16,412
the values are copied when the container starts.

17
00:01:16,412 --> 00:01:21,354
A later change to the ConfigMap is not seen until the Pod restarts.

18
00:01:21,354 --> 00:01:23,567
As files (a configMap volume):

19
00:01:23,567 --> 00:01:25,411
every key becomes a file.

20
00:01:25,411 --> 00:01:31,385
The kubelet updates the files when the ConfigMap changes (within about a minute),

21
00:01:31,385 --> 00:01:34,114
but the application must reread them.

22
00:01:35,867 --> 00:01:36,045
1.

23
00:01:36,045 --> 00:01:39,701
A namespace, the ConfigMaps and the Pods:

24
00:01:40,267 --> 00:01:40,405
2.

25
00:01:40,405 --> 00:01:47,786
A client Pod to call the others from inside the cluster (sleep keeps it running; exec runs commands in it):

26
00:01:48,333 --> 00:01:48,496
3.

27
00:01:48,496 --> 00:01:54,763
The backend reports the configuration it received (its /api/config endpoint):

28
00:01:54,763 --> 00:01:54,926
4.

29
00:01:54,926 --> 00:01:58,833
The frontend serves the page from the ConfigMap:

30
00:01:59,367 --> 00:01:59,516
4.

31
00:01:59,516 --> 00:02:03,096
The frontend serves the page from the ConfigMap:

32
00:02:04,833 --> 00:02:07,743
How do I see what Kubernetes did?

33
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:14,065
The variables inside the backend container, and the mounted files inside the frontend:

34
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:17,381
Let me try something.

35
00:02:17,381 --> 00:02:23,705
A Pod that asks for a key the ConfigMap does not have (GREETING instead of MESSAGE):

36
00:02:24,267 --> 00:02:25,776
What should happen?

37
00:02:25,776 --> 00:02:26,809
The Pod runs.

38
00:02:26,809 --> 00:02:27,922
What happened?

39
00:02:27,922 --> 00:02:30,067
CreateContainerConfigError:

40
00:02:30,067 --> 00:02:34,675
the kubelet could not build the container's configuration,

41
00:02:34,675 --> 00:02:38,886
so the container was never created (no logs to read).

42
00:02:40,633 --> 00:02:45,020
Add the missing key (or correct the reference in the Pod).

43
00:02:45,020 --> 00:02:48,878
The kubelet retries on its own, and the Pod starts:

44
00:02:50,633 --> 00:02:51,347
Got it.

45
00:02:51,347 --> 00:02:54,098
What do I need to remember?

46
00:02:54,633 --> 00:02:57,515
ConfigMaps keep configuration out of images:

47
00:02:57,515 --> 00:03:01,118
the same image runs everywhere with different settings.

48
00:03:01,667 --> 00:03:02,820
As variables:

49
00:03:02,820 --> 00:03:07,701
copied at container start (restart to pick up changes).

50
00:03:07,701 --> 00:03:08,500
As files:

51
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:11,251
kept up to date by the kubelet.

52
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:18,764
A wrong key or name gives CreateContainerConfigError; kubectl describe pod names the missing key.

53
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:23,285
I keep hearing about Secrets.

54
00:03:23,285 --> 00:03:25,109
What is it, really?

55
00:03:25,667 --> 00:03:29,487
A Secret is like a ConfigMap for sensitive values:

56
00:03:29,487 --> 00:03:32,697
passwords, API tokens, keys, certificates.

57
00:03:32,697 --> 00:03:37,969
Pods read it the same two ways, as environment variables or as files.

58
00:03:37,969 --> 00:03:41,484
The difference is in how Kubernetes treats it:

59
00:03:41,484 --> 00:03:45,381
Secrets are kept apart from ordinary configuration,

60
00:03:45,381 --> 00:03:49,202
can be protected separately with RBAC (lesson 24),

61
00:03:49,202 --> 00:03:56,461
are not printed by kubectl describe, and are only sent to nodes that run a Pod that needs them.

62
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:04,418
A password written in an image, a Git repository or a ConfigMap is readable by everyone who can read those.

63
00:04:04,418 --> 00:04:07,329
Secrets give credentials their own object,

64
00:04:07,329 --> 00:04:10,796
so that access to them can be limited and audited,

65
00:04:10,796 --> 00:04:15,856
and so that the same image can use different credentials per environment.

66
00:04:15,856 --> 00:04:18,629
> ⚠️ A Secret is encoded, not encrypted.

67
00:04:18,629 --> 00:04:24,245
Its values are stored base64-encoded, which anyone can decode with > one command.

68
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:28,420
And how does it actually work?

69
00:04:28,967 --> 00:04:36,818
stringData lets you write plain text in the YAML; Kubernetes encodes it into data when it stores the Secret.

70
00:04:36,818 --> 00:04:40,889
As a file (a secret volume) is safer than as a variable:

71
00:04:40,889 --> 00:04:47,142
variables are inherited by child processes and often end up in crash reports and logs.

72
00:04:47,142 --> 00:04:54,775
Mounted Secrets live in a memory file system (tmpfs) on the node and are updated when the Secret changes.

73
00:04:54,775 --> 00:05:02,481
kubectl describe secret shows only the sizes of the values; kubectl get secret -o yaml shows them encoded.

74
00:05:04,233 --> 00:05:04,399
1.

75
00:05:04,399 --> 00:05:07,873
The namespace, the Secret and the backend:

76
00:05:08,433 --> 00:05:08,572
2.

77
00:05:08,572 --> 00:05:15,238
A client Pod, and the backend's view of its configuration (it shows the password only as "set"):

78
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,909
How do I see what Kubernetes did?

79
00:05:20,467 --> 00:05:22,866
describe hides the values:

80
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:26,428
Let me try something.

81
00:05:26,428 --> 00:05:32,084
A typo in the Secret's name (db-credential, without the final s):

82
00:05:32,633 --> 00:05:34,361
What should happen?

83
00:05:34,361 --> 00:05:35,543
The Pod runs.

84
00:05:35,543 --> 00:05:36,816
What happened?

85
00:05:36,816 --> 00:05:39,271
CreateContainerConfigError:

86
00:05:39,271 --> 00:05:44,818
the container was never created, so kubectl logs has nothing.

87
00:05:46,567 --> 00:05:50,030
A Pod's containers cannot be changed after creation:

88
00:05:50,030 --> 00:05:52,961
delete it and create it with the right name.

89
00:05:54,700 --> 00:05:55,413
Got it.

90
00:05:55,413 --> 00:05:58,164
What do I need to remember?

91
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:06,119
Secrets hold sensitive configuration; Pods read them as variables or (better) as files.

92
00:06:06,667 --> 00:06:15,356
base64 is an encoding, not encryption; Secrets are unencrypted in etcd unless encryption at rest is enabled.

93
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:22,689
Wrong name or key → CreateContainerConfigError; kubectl describe pod names what is missing.
